   The whole apartment cut out at once: laptop, lamp, router, fan, one
clean silence. In the dark, the dancers stayed lit for two seconds more, a
ballroom hanging on my wall in blue and gold, nobody’s light, no pro‐
jector’s. Then they went too, and I stood there listening to the radiators
tick back to normal like nothing had happened, because as far as the
radiators were concerned, nothing had.
   Two flat taps on my door. Mamá’s knock: one to ask permission, one
to inform me of her decision.
   “Juan Carlos.” Through the wood, not a question. “Se fue la luz.”
   “It’s the whole block, Má.” I hadn’t checked. I said it the way you
say things to your mother at three in the morning, with total confid‐
ence and no evidence.
   A pause. I could hear the rosary, the small clack of it, in her fist. I
could hear her not asking.
   “Acuéstate,” she said finally. Go to bed. Then footsteps, going away,
the rosary going with them, and I listened for her bedroom door and
didn’t hear it, because she’d stopped at the end of the hall. She does
that when she’s decided not to ask. She can stand there a full minute
and say more than most people say in a week.
   I went to the window.
   The whole block was dark. Streetlights, the bodega sign, the laun‐
dromat across Jerome, every window up and down the street, all of it
out, like somebody had thumbed the block off. All of it except one: the
third floor of the empty building across from ours, the one wrapped in
blue construction netting since fall, where nobody had lived in a year
and a half. One window up there was lit, a flat white that didn’t flicker.
A man stood in it, not moving, headphones on, facing our building the
way you face a radio when the game’s close.
   I had built a chismosa to listen to my building. It had never crossed
my mind that somebody else might be listening to the same pipe.




